{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1787920830007,"sessionID":"ses_fb7ab68c8ffedzLU3MljFj4aDT","part":{"id":"prt_04862ce26001tzQ59vUdUpzwar","messageID":"msg_04862baf1001f4sUCx9V18wiT6","sessionID":"ses_fb7ab68c8ffedzLU3MljFj4aDT","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787920834680,"sessionID":"ses_fb7ab68c8ffedzLU3MljFj4aDT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch_web_search_exa","callID":"websearch_web_search_exa_3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"numResults":1,"query":"noop2"},"output":"Title: yoshuawuyts/noop2\nURL: https://github.com/yoshuawuyts/noop2/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# yoshuawuyts/noop2\n...\nNo operation as a module™\n...\n# noop2\n...\nNo operation as a module™. The long awaited successor to [`noop`][noop1]. Noop2\ndoes even less than its predecessor, and is 100% tested to guarantee nothing\nhappens.\n...\n```bash\nnpm install noop2\n```\n...\n```js\nvar noop = require('noop2');\n\nnoop();\n// nothing happened\n```\n...\n## Why?\n...\nThis is both a joke and actually useful. Obviously we _might_ be taking npm\na bit too far by bundling this as a module, but when running tests it's\nsometimes nice to be able to import an empty function if only to keep style\nconsistent. So here you have it.\n...\n- [the original noop][noop1]\n- [nop][nop]\n- [no-op][no-op]","metadata":{"truncated":false},"title":"","time":{"start":1787920832534,"end":1787920834647}},"id":"prt_04862d80d001j8a5NfzRBH4Ptf","sessionID":"ses_fb7ab68c8ffedzLU3MljFj4aDT","messageID":"msg_04862baf1001f4sUCx9V18wiT6"}}
